{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310738,"nonce":"Dz/ccII7OxZi1w/Aolanea8L2o4=","title":"Cranelift backend emits a 4-byte udf trap stub for any function it can't compile, and CASCADES the failure to every caller — one MIR-level ...","message":"**Status: documented; root mechanism is intentional but needs better diagnostics.**\n\n`compiler/src/codegen/cranelift_backend.rs::compile_module_without_finalize`\n(around lines 811-928) installs a **trap stub** for any function in\nthree cases:\n\n1. **Generic template not yet monomorphised** (line 821) — unresolved\n   type params, emits `define_trap_stub` + adds to `failed_funcs`.\n2. **Calls a failed function** (line 850-861, \"cascade\") — transitive\n   poison: if any callee is in `failed_funcs`, the caller becomes a\n   stub too. Repeats until fixpoint.\n3. **`compile_function` itself errored** (line 912-927, \"compile-fail\")\n   — MIR-level inconsistencies (e.g. `Argument IrId(N) not found in\n   value_map`, return-type vs `Return with no value` mismatches).\n\n**The trap stub is 4 bytes**: a single `udf #0xc11f` instruction\n(Cranelift's universal trap marker — \"CLIF\" in hex). The function's\npointer is then registered at that 4-byte location, and any direct\ncall lands on the trap → `EXC_BAD_INSTRUCTION` at runtime.\n\n**Symptom**: A program SIGILLs (exit 132) at startup with no runtime\ntrace output. The crash PC is page-aligned (e.g. 0x102f10000) with a\nnormal function prologue (`pacibsp`) at +4 — pattern of \"we're\ncalling a function that compiled to nothing but a tombstone\".\n\n**Diagnosis** — set `RAYZOR_DUMP_FN_PTRS=1` to see:\n- Every function pointer install with its address.\n- `[trap-stub:generic] ...` for unresolved generics.\n- `[trap-stub:cascade] ... calls failed: [...]` for the cascade.\n- `[trap-stub:compile-fail] ...: \u003cerror\u003e` for actual codegen errors —\n  *this is the bug-finder*, the original compile-fail message tells\n  you what broke.\n\n(Added in this session under the `RAYZOR_DUMP_FN_PTRS` env var — see\nalso `RAYZOR_DUMP_CLIF=\u003csubstr\u003e` for per-function CLIF dumps. These\nare the MVP of the `project_debugger_roadmap` Phase 1 tool.)\n\n**Three real compile-fail patterns found this session** (each got\nits own bug note + nue-side workaround):\n\n1. **`bugs_switch_with_let_field_scrutinee`** — switch-as-expression\n   case body with `var x = …; expr;` shape; trailing expr was\n   dropped. *FIXED at compiler level (commit 9045bcb).*\n2. **`bugs_divergent_throw_in_switch_expr`** — switch-as-expression\n   default case is `throw \"...\"`; codegen emits `Return` without\n   value because the throw block never falls through. *Worked\n   around in nue (lift the throw out of the switch); compiler fix\n   TBD.*\n3. **`bugs_multi_value_case_clause`** — `case 12, 14:` only matches\n   the first literal; second value falls through to default.\n   *Worked around in nue (split into `case 12: ... case 14: ...`);\n   compiler fix TBD; thought-to-be fixed by b6f588b but evidently\n   regressed or only partial.*\n\n**The cascade design is reasonable** (don't try to compile callers\nof broken callees → they'll just fail too) but the **silent\nSIGILL is brutal UX**. Improvements worth landing:\n\n- Emit a compile-time warning listing every trap-stubbed function\n  with its reason. Currently the only signal is the `debug!` log\n  which isn't visible at default verbosity.\n- Make `compile-fail` errors fatal by default, with `--allow-trap-stubs`\n  to recover the current permissive behaviour. Trap-stubbing should\n  be opt-in, not silent.\n- For each trap-stub, register source-info so the runtime can\n  produce a \"Called function X was trap-stubbed at compile time\n  because Y\" message instead of `EXC_BAD_INSTRUCTION`.\n\nRelated: [[project_debugger_roadmap]] Phase 1; [[bugs_switch_with_let_field_scrutinee]];\n[[bugs_gguf_tensor_decode_crash]] (this is the resolution of that\ninvestigation).\n\n\nA specific subtype: adding any new top-level type ref (`import nue.concurrent.WorkerPool`,\n`import nue.sampling.TemperatureSampler`) into a nue.* module that's already in the\nBLADE cache shifts class IDs at save time, leaving stale ids in cached maps that\ncannot be re-keyed by qualified name at load time. Manifests as silent exit-0 or\nSIGSEGV/SIGILL at the first call into the affected code path.\n\nWorkflow `woa9qquvk` attempted to fix this via two changes in `compilation.rs`:\n\n1. Move `interface_vtables` restoration above other restorations in `restore_cached_maps`\n   and re-resolve each cached method qname against `import_class_method_symbols` keyed\n   by `(impl_class_sym, method_name)`. Sound in isolation; closes one specific subpath\n   (vtable slot resolution).\n\n2. Add `fixup_stale_stdlib_function_ids` symmetric with `fixup_stale_constructor_ids`,\n   walking `stdlib_function_map` and re-keying func_ids from `stdlib_function_name_map`\n   once all imports are loaded. Fires correctly on at least one stale entry\n   (`haxe.ds.TreeNode.get_height: 180002 → 190000`).\n\n**Both fixes landed clean but the cascade STILL reproduces** on `/tmp/trap_repro/repro.sh`\nSTEP 2. The real culprit lives in an un-audited map. Candidates per workflow Phase 3:\n\n- `import_function_param_iface_names`\n- `external_function_param_types`\n- CallDirect targets in cached MIR not present in `external_function_names`\n\nCompounded by [[bugs-cache-hit-fast-path-bypasses-fixups]] — `src/main.rs:1054-1078`\ntakes a cache-hit fast path that bypasses ALL fixups when source hash matches. So\neven a perfect fixup pass in compilation.rs cannot rescue a poisoned `Main.mir.cache`.\n\nThe 104 LOC from workflow woa9qquvk were reverted (correct in isolation but add\nmisleading machinery without closing the bug; future investigators should see a\nclean slate).\n\n\nPer workflow synthesis: instrument codegen entry of `LlamaModel.forwardIds`\nto print every `CallDirect` target `IrFunctionId`. Run with cache cold (STEP 1) AND\nwarm (STEP 2). Diff the two ID streams — the id whose resolved real function differs\nidentifies the culprit map. Then add a `fixup_stale_*_ids` for that specific map AND\neither invalidate cache on import-set change or run fixups on cached MIR before\nexecution.\n\n\n- WorkerPool / TemperatureSampler imports in nue.Linear (per [[project-qmatmul-threaded]]\n  and [[project-llama-chat-temperature-penalty]])\n- Llama 3.1 8B Q4_K_M SIGSEGV at `[gen] streaming...` (per [[bugs-llama-8b-sigsegv-at-gen-start]])\n  — 8B's vocab loop triggers `haxe.ds.TreeNode` / `BalancedTree` / iterator instantiation\n  paths that 1B doesn't, surfacing the cascade via `__reflect_ctor_wrap_XXX calls failed`\n\n\n**Root cause identified.** Diagnostic instrumentation at `cranelift_backend.rs:2613`\n(CallDirect arm) emitting `[CALLDIRECT] caller_fn target_id resolved_name` for both\ncold (STEP1, PASS) and warm-cache (STEP2, SIGSEGV) runs, then normalised diff:\n\n- 0 unique STEP2 (caller_fn, resolved_name) pairs\n- 19 unique STEP1 pairs — all TemperatureSampler-internal\n\n→ **Codegen names every callee correctly on warm cache.** `cl_func_id` renumbering\nis benign. The trap-stub set is identical between STEP1 and STEP2 per\n`RAYZOR_DUMP_FN_PTRS=1` comparison; the 5 generic trap-stubs on `haxe.iterators.*`\n+ `haxe.ds.TreeNode.new` + `BalancedTree.new` + `__reflect_ctor_wrap_472` are\nPRE-EXISTING benign trap-stubs unrelated to the user-visible SIGSEGV.\n\n**The cascade is a RUNTIME ABI mismatch at the call boundary**, not a misresolved\nCallDirect target. Surfaces inside `forward()` after `[build] LlamaModel` finishes.\n\n\nThree SymbolId-keyed iface maps consumed by\n`hir_to_mir.rs::maybe_materialize_for_call` are silently empty on warm-cache restore:\n\n1. `import_interface_method_names`\n2. `import_interface_method_return_types` — **dominant**, drives Dynamic→concrete\n   return-type re-resolve per [[bugs-iface-method-return-dynamic]]\n3. `import_interface_extends`\n\nOnly `interface_vtables` got BLADE persistence in commit `c7a170d` (the iface-to-iface\ncast fix). The three sibling maps were missed. On warm cache:\n\n- class→iface fat-ptr wrap doesn't fire\n- iface-method return type can't be re-resolved (falls back to `Dynamic` / `Any`)\n- downstream first-tensor-op reads garbage and SIGSEGVs\n\nMatches the observed crash boundary exactly: build completes, forward fails before\nany `[forward]` print.\n\n\n- `import_function_param_iface_names` already persists (compilation.rs:3188 restore +\n  4961 save) + renumbered at 3603-3609.\n- `external_function_param_types` is rebuilt fresh every compile from\n  `import_mir_modules` (compilation.rs:4732-4752), cannot go stale.\n- Stale CallDirect targets — ruled out by 0 STEP2-only normalised pairs.\n- Trap-stub set — identical STEP1 vs STEP2.\n\nThe actual culprit is a fourth, previously-unlisted hypothesis: the three iface-method\nSIBLING maps to `interface_vtables`, all missing from BLADE persistence.\n\n\n1. `compiler/src/ir/blade.rs`: add 3 qname-keyed entry types\n   (`BladeInterfaceMethodNamesEntry`, `BladeInterfaceMethodReturnTypeEntry`,\n   `BladeInterfaceExtendsEntry`) + 3 fields on `BladeCachedMaps`.\n2. `compiler/src/compilation.rs::build_cached_maps_from_mir_result` (lines 817-1034):\n   accept the three MIR maps; resolve `SymbolId`→qualified class/interface names and\n   `TypeId`→qualified type names using the same closures already used for the\n   `iface_vtable` case at 992-1029.\n3. Call site at `compilation.rs:4828`: pass `\u0026mir_result.interface_method_names`,\n   `\u0026mir_result.interface_method_return_types`, `\u0026mir_result.interface_extends`.\n4. `compilation.rs::restore_cached_maps`: add three for-loops immediately after the\n   `iface_vtables` restore at 3289-3320; resolve qnames back to ids via the same\n   `registered` map; use the `all_found` gate (silent partial-skip; NEVER panic).\n\nNO `fixup_stale_*` needed (SymbolIds are stable in-session via\n`register_symbols_from_type_info`). NO `hir_to_mir.rs` change (consumers already\nhandle empty maps; the patch just stops the maps being empty). NO backend change.\n\n\n- STEP2 of `/tmp/trap_repro/repro.sh` exits 0 with `=== PASS ===`\n- 128/128 haxe regression stays green\n- tiny-transformer smoke stays green\n- `tools/llama-diff/compare.sh` stays MATCH\n- 8B SIGSEGV at `[gen] streaming...` should also unblock (same root cause class)\n\n\n**A new class of cause** beyond the iface-sibling persistence gap and the\nimport-cascade subtype above: cross-module `CallDirect` targets pointing\ninto stdlib modules that get tree-shaken between cache save and cache\nrestore (`haxe.ds.*`, `haxe.Exception`, `StringBuf`-adjacent). The\nsaved IrFunctionId survives renumbering by raw value but the underlying\nmodule is gone, so resolution falls into an empty stub, and the JIT\nsilently emits a W0020 trap-stub. Downstream callers cascade per the\nmechanism above.\n\n**Fix landing (2026-06-03):** extend `fixup_stale_cross_module_refs` in\n`compiler/src/compilation.rs` to walk `user_mir_modules` (not just its\nprior scopes) and re-resolve CallDirect targets by qualified name via\n`stdlib_function_name_map`, mirroring `fixup_stale_constructor_ids` /\n`fixup_stale_method_ids`. Pair with **path E**: a post-fixup strict\nassert that any still-unresolved cross-module CallDirect becomes a\nloud `E0600 CallDirect target IrFunctionId(N) in \u003cfn\u003e is unresolvable`\ncompile error instead of a silent W0020 trap-stub. Removes the entire\nclass of silent-SIGSEGV downstream — the streaming-decode \"heap\ncorruption\" failure mode in\n[[bugs-llama-chat-streaming-heap-corruption]] is closed by this fix.\n\n**Caveat:** the fix closes the class for callees discoverable in\n`stdlib_function_name_map` + `user_mir_modules`. A residual cold-cache\nSIGSEGV at `metadataFromReader` / `GGUFTokenizer.build` (`IrFunctionId(200005)`)\nstill surfaces on llama-chat — same bug class, but the stale target\nlives in a map not yet covered by the walk. Path E correctly surfaces\nit once before reproducing flakily on fresh cache.\n\n\nA subtler subtype of the cross-module CallDirect cause above. `stdlib_function_name_map` is seeded during user-package compile from **pre-merge** local stub IrFunctionIds taken directly from `mir_module.functions`. After the stdlib merge into the user package, `id_replacements` renumbers those local stubs to merged-canonical ids — but the name map's VALUES are NEVER refreshed by that renumber. The secondary `fixup_stale_cross_module_refs` pass then trusts the (stale) map as an oracle and OVERWRITES correct cross-module CallDirects with deleted-stub ids (e.g. `fn470007 → fn12`).\n\nSymptom: with Path E on, surfaces as `E0600 CallDirect target IrFunctionId(N) in \u003cfn\u003e is unresolvable` in user-package callers (commonly `nue.loader.GGUFTokenizer.{build,collectStrings,readFloatArrayOr}`, `nue.transformer.{GeluFFN.parameters, SwiGLU.forward}`). With Path E off (the W0020 trap-stub permissive default), surfaces as silent SIGILL during decode — the original \"60% streaming heap corruption\" mode from\n[[bugs-llama-chat-streaming-heap-corruption]].\n\n**Fix landing (2026-06-03):** apply `id_replacements` to `stdlib_function_name_map`'s VALUES at the same point the renumber runs against `mir_module.functions`, after the Main.hx stdlib merge. Plus harden Sweep 1 of the validation pass to NOT count `cached_stdlib_mir` ids as \"live\" when deciding whether to drop a name-map entry (cached entries can shadow user-merged bodies that haven't been renumbered yet). Cold-cache llama-chat goes 0/10 → 4/10 with `residual_e0600=[]`, `match_paris=4/10`, bench=2.402 tok/s Cranelift. Remaining 6/10 failures are a different bug class.\n\nDiagnostic recipe: before this fix, `RAYZOR_DUMP_FN_PTRS=1` + Path E surfaced 5 E0600s pointing at low local-stub ids (12, 412) that the user caller could not possibly intend to call directly — that shape (low IrFunctionId in user-module CallDirect rewrites) is the signature of this cause class.\n\n\nA non-determinism probe compared the clean-build trap-stub set against the incremental-build trap-stub set on the post-b013900 working tree, targeting the two W0020 residuals from `bugs_llama_chat_streaming_heap_corruption.md` trace-diff:\n\n- `IrFunctionId(280007)` (caller: `nue.arch.{LlamaArch,BertArch}.build`) — **absent in both builds**\n- `IrFunctionId(220015)` (caller: `nue.loader.GGUFTokenizer.build`) — **absent in both builds**\n- novel ids unique to clean: `[]`\n- novel ids unique to incremental: `[]`\n\n→ **b013900 is correct in its scope and NOT to be rolled back.** The commit attacks the stdlib_function_name_map stale-stub-id rewrite gap (stdlib callees) and closes it. Both build states verify this — neither produces a 280007 or 220015 trap-stub *of stdlib origin*.\n\nThe residual 280007/220015 trap-stubs that DO appear in some failing-run trap-stub dumps live in a DIFFERENT map: the user→user CallDirect renumber path. `stdlib_function_name_map` covers stdlib targets only; `nue.arch.LlamaArch.build`'s callee at IrFunctionId(280007) is a user→user edge that b013900's rewrite never inspects.\n\n\nSymmetric mirror of the stdlib path, ~80 LOC:\n\n1. `compiler/src/compilation.rs`: build `user_function_name_map: HashMap\u003cString, IrFunctionId\u003e` keyed by qualified name → final renumbered id. Populate as user modules merge, applying `id_replacements` to values at the same point `stdlib_function_name_map` gets renumbered (the same site b013900 modified).\n2. Extend `fixup_stale_cross_module_refs` to walk user→user CallDirect targets against `user_function_name_map` after the stdlib branch runs. Mirror the qname-fallback shape.\n3. `compiler/src/codegen/cranelift_backend.rs::compile_module` (≈line 929): on \"Called function IrFunctionId(N) not found in module\", last-resort lookup against `merged_modules.func_id_to_qualified_name` + `bare_name_to_id_set` before installing the trap-stub. Only stub if the fallback also misses.\n\nNO rollback of b013900. NO new BLADE persistence (different cause class from the iface-sibling gap closed by 214ed60). The 280007/220015 cluster is a user-module post-merge renumber gap, not a cache-persistence gap.\n\n\n`RAYZOR_DUMP_FN_PTRS=1 rayzor run nue/examples/llama-chat/Main.hx -- \u003cgguf\u003e \"test\" 100 4096 0.01` on a fail run. The trap-stub dump must surface the qualified name 280007 and 220015 were trying to reach. That tells us which user→user edge to fix first.\n\n---\n\n\nThe recipe from `w7c228293` landed shape-correctly as `214ed60` — 222 LOC\nadding `BladeInterfaceMethodNamesEntry/_ReturnType/_Extends` qname-keyed\npersistence in `blade.rs` + matching `build_cached_maps_from_mir_result`\nextension + 3 restore loops in `restore_cached_maps`. Slot-alignment\ndiscipline tightened (`method_names` skipped entirely if any interner\nlookup fails, matching the c7a170d `interface_extends` pattern).\n\n**BUT THE CASCADE STILL FIRES.** Both `/tmp/trap_repro/repro.sh` STEP2\nAND 8B Q4_K_M generation still silently EXIT=0 after `[build]` /\n`[gen] streaming...`. The iface-sibling persistence was a real\ncorrectness gap (now closed) but NOT the cascade root cause.\n\n**8B failure mode CHANGED** with this fix: was EXIT=139 (SIGSEGV);\nis now EXIT=0 (silent return). Same shape as the cascade-repro\nsilent-return pattern. Loader + tokenizer paths fine (1.038s load,\n17 sane prompt tokens). The downstream is still broken.\n\n\nPer workflow w38cm0vcw synthesis:\n\n1. Run `RAYZOR_DUMP_FN_PTRS=1 /tmp/trap_repro/repro.sh` STEP2.\n2. Identify which `IrFunctionId` is trap-stubbing in `forward()`.\n3. Likely candidates:\n   - Cross-module ref to `LlamaArch.build`-adjacent symbol\n   - BLADE-cached function body referencing renumbered `IrFunctionId`\n     that the post-restore fixup doesn't catch\n   - Per-method MIR re-resolve gap\n\nCombine with the already-landed cache-key import-set hash (5186eb8) +\nthe iface-sibling persistence (214ed60); whichever stale id remains\nshould now be the entire universe of broken state.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_trap_stub_cascade` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}